                {
                  "obligationId": "scenario%3Afile-owner-without-destination-write-grant::entry%3Apost-process-file",
                  "decision": {
                    "kind": "observed",
                    "observed": "allow"
                  },
                  "branchResults": [],
                  "explanation": "The declared entry authenticates a verified user and, for a non-admin, authorizes access to the source by loading the file with both file ID and user ID. That is a source-file ownership check only. The caller-provided collection_name is then bound directly as the destination. On the collection-name path, the endpoint obtains document content from the owned file or its per-file vector collection, and—unless embedding/retrieval is bypassed—calls save_docs_to_vector_db with that destination and add=True. In save_docs_to_vector_db, an existing collection is not rejected when add=True; after duplicate-content and ordinary processing conditions, VECTOR_DB_CLIENT.insert writes the generated items to that collection. No destination ownership or explicit destination write-grant check is invoked on this path before the insert. Although the supplied source defines _validate_collection_access, which can enforce an access type by rejecting denied collections, process_file does not call it in the displayed declared-entry flow. Thus, under the stated premise that the verified non-admin owns the source file but has no ownership or write grant for the existing selected destination, the source-visible authorization behavior allows the destination write. Operational conditions can independently prevent a particular insertion—for example BYPASS_EMBEDDING_AND_RETRIEVAL returns before the sink, empty content can fail, or the duplicate-hash check can reject content associated with a different file—but these are not destination authorization checks and do not cure the authorization path.",
                  "facts": [
